[0.007, "o", "\r\n==============================================================================\r\nQ: Why did signups drop in mid-June 2026?\r\n==============================================================================\r\n\r\nConfidence: insufficient evidence\r\nTook 101s over 11 steps, 35,058 tokens, 23 observations gathered, 18 cited\r\n\r\nANSWER\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups genuinely and durably dropped by roughly 55%, from ~210-230/day to ~93-107/day, with the change occurring sharply on 2026-06-17 and persisting through the end of the available data on 2026-06-29.  [posthog.event_trend 4c446542]\r\n\r\nFINDINGS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  Signups dropped sharply and durably starting June 17  (high)\r\n    - Daily signups averaged roughly 210-230 from 2026-05-15 through 2026-06-16.  [posthog.event_trend 4c446542]\r\n    - From 2026-06-17 onward daily signups fell to roughly 93-107 per day and remained at that lower level "]
[0.000, "o", "through 2026-06-29, a decline of about 55%.  [posthog.event_trend 4c446542]\r\n  No corresponding engineering, deploy, or communications activity found in any connected source  (medium)\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/product between 2026-06-08 and 2026-06-22, nor in the wider 2026-06-01 to 2026-07-01 window.  [github.commits 62972f11, github.commits 231e0260]\r\n    - No commits were found in acme/company-website, acme/marketing-site, or acme/mobile between 2026-06-10 and 2026-06-20.  [github.commits a853a5e4, github.commits 52ea066d, github.commits 3cc03f0a]\r\n    - No deployments to either the web-preview or web-live environment of acme/product were recorded in the window searched, though the deployment history mechanism does record deployments outside that window, so this is a window gap rather than confirmation deploys never happen.  [github.deployment_history 3d3eebed, github.deployment_history 2284479e, github.deployment_history c9f591b9]\r\n    - No pull requests of any state were found for acme/product"]
[0.000, "o", ", and no GitHub issues were opened in acme/product since 2026-06-10.  [github.recent_prs 5e4da232, github.issues c3ff2788]\r\n    - No Slack messages matching 'signup drop', 'signups', or 'onboarding broken' were found, no PostHog annotation matched 'signup', no active or inactive feature flag matched 'signup', no experiments are running, and no Slack decision was found on the topic of signup conversion.  [slack.search_messages b7788c43, slack.search_messages e65421dc, slack.search_messages 2c9eea06, posthog.annotations ef808c34, posthog.feature_flags d08bf57d, posthog.experiments cba049e9, slack.find_decision a8ef00a9]\r\n\r\nCHARTS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  user signed up over time\r\n  value\r\n    value              |▇▇▆▇▇██▇█▇▇▇▆█▇▇██▇█▇▇▇████▇▇▇▇▇▇▁▁▁▁▁▂▁▁▁▂▁▂▁|  93 → 230\r\n                        2026-05-15 … 2026-06-29\r\n\r\n\r\nHYPOTHESES TESTED\r\n-------------------"]
[0.000, "o", "-----------------------------------------------------------\r\n  [inconclusive] A code change or deploy to acme/product around 2026-06-17 caused the signup drop.\r\n      No commits, PRs, or deployments were found in the relevant windows, but the deployment/commit tools returned explicitly empty results rather than a confirmed absence of activity outside the queried windows, so this cannot be ruled in or out from what was retrieved.\r\n  [inconclusive] A change to the marketing site, company website, or mobile client drove the drop.\r\n      No commits were found in any of these repos in the surrounding window, but this reflects the absence of visible commit activity in that window rather than a confirmed absence of any change; no other change record (deploy, PR) was available to test this against.\r\n  [contradicted] The event was discussed or flagged internally (an incident, a known regression, a flag flip) and would surface in Slack, annotations, or feature flags.\r\n      Every search of Slack, PostHog annotations, a"]
[0.000, "o", "nd feature flags for signup-related terms returned zero matches, so there is no record in these tools of the drop being noticed, flagged, or explained by an internal actor.\r\n\r\nRECOMMENDATIONS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  P1 Widen the GitHub deployment_history and commit queries to a longer lookback (e.g. 2026-05-01 to 2026-07-01) and check other environments or infra-level changes (e.g. DNS, third-party auth provider, payment processor) not visible to these connectors.\r\n      why: All engineering-side sources returned empty for the immediate window around the onset, which rules out nothing definitively -- the actual change may sit just outside the queried range or in a system not connected here.\r\n      [github.commits 231e0260, github.deployment_history 3d3eebed]\r\n  P1 Break down signups by acquisition channel, platform, or referrer for the week before and after 2026-06-17 using whatever properties PostHog does capture on the 'user signed up' event.\r\n     "]
[0.000, "o", " why: The aggregate trend confirms the drop but does not identify whether it is concentrated in one channel or platform, which would point directly at a cause (e.g. a specific ad campaign ending, or one platform's signup flow breaking).\r\n      [posthog.event_trend 4c446542]\r\n\r\nRISKS AND CAVEATS\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - The empty results across github.commits, github.recent_prs, and github.deployment_history for the relevant windows are consistent with a genuine absence of activity, but are equally consistent with a repository history that this connector cannot see fully, or a window filter that excluded the actual change. The true cause of the onset may exist in a source or time range not queried here.\r\n  - No breakdown of the signup drop by segment, channel, or platform was available -- posthog.event_trend with a $current_url breakdown returned an unbroken aggregate series, so it is not known whether the drop is uniform across web/mobile/channel or "]
[0.000, "o", "concentrated in one segment.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) were removed because an independent check found the cited evidence did not support them. Treat the remaining conclusions with corresponding caution.\r\n  - 1 claim(s) go beyond their evidence and are retained with reduced confidence rather than removed, because the rest of each one holds. What the check found:\r\n- executive_summary[1]: Commits were checked across all four repos, but pull requests, issues, and deployment history were only queried for acme/product, not the other three repositories, so the claim of \r\n  - 1 causal claim(s) were withheld: a separate check of the evidence gathered, made without seeing this report, found that it does not support a definitive answer about cause. Every query for commits, deployments, PRs, issues, experiments, flags, annotations, and Slack discussion returned zero rows, and the one trend series shown does not clearly demonstrate the drop itself within the visible truncated window. There is no dated candidate cause here at all, "]
[0.000, "o", "so no connection between any change and the movement can be drawn from this evidence. What would settle it: A signup trend series showing a clear drop in mid-June (the trend data shown only extends stably through early June without a visible decline); Deployment or commit records covering the actual onset window if it falls outside the queried date ranges; Any dated candidate cause (config change, marketing campaign end, pricing change, third-party outage) that precedes and aligns with the onset of the drop; Confirmation that the tracki\r\n\r\nDATA QUALITY\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  - posthog.event_trend with breakdown_property='$current_url' returned an unbroken aggregate series (breakdown_property came back null in the response), so no segment-level view of the drop was actually obtained despite the request.\r\n  - Deployment history explicitly notes that acme/product does record deployments outside the queried window, so the empty deployment results should "]
[0.000, "o", "not be read as 'no deploys ever' -- only as 'none found in this window'.\r\n  - 17 of the 18 cited observation(s) returned no results (github.commits, github.deployment_history, github.issues, github.recent_prs, posthog.annotations, posthog.experiments, posthog.feature_flags, slack.find_decision, slack.search_messages). Conclusions resting on them describe what was not found, which is not the same as what does not exist.\r\n\r\nSOURCES\r\n------------------------------------------------------------------------------\r\n  62972f11  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  231e0260  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  a853a5e4  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  52ea066d  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  3cc03f0a  github.commits  eval://github__commits\r\n  3d3eebed  github.deployment_history  eval://github__deployment_history\r\n  2284479e  github.deployment_history  eval://github__deployment_history\r\n  c9f591b9  github.deployment_history  eval://github__deployment_history\r\n  c3ff2788  github.issues  "]
[0.000, "o", "eval://github__issues\r\n  5e4da232  github.recent_prs  eval://github__recent_prs\r\n  ef808c34  posthog.annotations  eval://posthog__annotations\r\n  4c446542  posthog.event_trend  eval://posthog__event_trend\r\n  cba049e9  posthog.experiments  eval://posthog__experiments\r\n  d08bf57d  posthog.feature_flags  eval://posthog__feature_flags\r\n  a8ef00a9  slack.find_decision  eval://slack__find_decision\r\n  b7788c43  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  e65421dc  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n  2c9eea06  slack.search_messages  eval://slack__search_messages\r\n"]
